We need to update this API based on feedback in #50037 that the `/manage/info` endpoint throws an `ArgumentException` when a user has multiple roles.

## Background and Motivation

`InfoResponse.Claims` should support multiple values per claim type since this is commonly used. Roles are one extremely common use case where multiple claims will have the same `ClaimsIdentity.RoleClaimType`, but multiple claims with the same type

## Proposed API

```diff
// Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.dll
namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.Data;

public sealed class InfoResponse
{
    public required string Email { get; init; }
    public required bool IsEmailConfirmed { get; init; }
-    public required IDictionary<string, string> Claims { get; init; }
+    public required IDictionary<string, string[]> Claims { get; init; }
}
```

#50953 targets release/8.0 and implements this updated API.

## Usage Examples

```csharp
    private static async Task<InfoResponse> CreateInfoResponseAsync<TUser>(TUser user, ClaimsPrincipal claimsPrincipal, UserManager<TUser> userManager)
        where TUser : class
    {
        return new()
        {
            Email = await userManager.GetEmailAsync(user) ?? throw new NotSupportedException("Users must have an email."),
            IsEmailConfirmed = await userManager.IsEmailConfirmedAsync(user),
            Claims = AggregateDictionary(claimsPrincipal.Claims.Select(claim => (claim.Type, claim.Value))),
        };
    }

    private static Dictionary<TKey, TValue[]> AggregateDictionary<TKey, TValue>(IEnumerable<(TKey, TValue)> pairs) where TKey : notnull
    {
        var dictionary = new Dictionary<TKey, TValue[]>(1);
        foreach (var (key, value) in pairs)
        {
            TValue[] newDescriptions;

            if (dictionary.TryGetValue(key, out var descriptions))
            {
                newDescriptions = new TValue[descriptions.Length + 1];
                Array.Copy(descriptions, newDescriptions, descriptions.Length);
                newDescriptions[descriptions.Length] = value;
            }
            else
            {
                newDescriptions = [value];
            }

            dictionary[key] = newDescriptions;
        }

        return dictionary;
    }
```

## Alternative Designs

We could also make the `Claims` an array rather than a dictionary/object which would be more similar to `ClaimsPrincipal.Claims`.

```diff
// Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.dll
namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.Data;

public sealed class InfoResponse
{
    public required string Email { get; init; }
    public required bool IsEmailConfirmed { get; init; }
-    public required IDictionary<string, string> Claims { get; init; }
+    public required ClaimInfo[] Claims { get; init; }
}

+ public sealed class ClaimInfo
+ {
+    public required string Type { get; init; }
+    public required string Value { get; init; }
+ }
```

Or we could continue to use a dictionary, but use the concrete `Dictionary<string, string[]>` type instead of `IDictionary<string, string[]>` interface the implementation is probably always going to be a normal `Dictionary` going forward.

## Risks

The biggest risk is doing nothing and leaving the `/manage/info` completely broken for any user with multiple roles.
